        <body>
            <div xml:id="d95">

                <pb facs="0203" n="119" sameAs="frus:frus1903/pg119"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Conger to Mr.
                            Hay.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1901-10-16"><hi rendition="#italic">October 16,
                        1901</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 779.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to transmit herewith
                    translations of correspondence between the foreign office and this legation upon
                    the question <pb facs="0204" n="120" xml:id="pg120"/> of the continued residence
                    in Peking of foreign merchants who located here during its occupation by the
                    allied forces. * * *</p>
                <p>The treaties do not permit the residence of any foreign merchants in the city of
                    Peking, but several years ago permission was given for the opening of stores
                    here by a Dane, a German, and a Frenchman for the especial accommodation of the
                    legations, but their trade soon developed into a very large business with the
                    Chinese. The Hongkong and Shanghai and the Russo-Chinese banks have also been
                    established here for several years, so that the city has, in fact, by precedent
                    at least, been opened to special if not to general trade.</p>
                <p>It is true that the banks and stores mentioned are located within the limits of
                    the legation quarter, but their business extends throughout the city. I have
                    consequently, as have most of my colleagues, thought best to keep the question
                    open, with the hope that its final settlement would result in opening the city
                    generally to foreign trade. Further action will be promptly reported.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">E. H. Conger.</hi></signed></closer>



